WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Embeddings and indexes are sensitive infrastructure in a private RAG system.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.080
An embedding is a mathematical representation used to support similarity search,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.015
but it is derived from source content.

00:00:10.015 --> 00:00:13.380
When the source material is sensitive, the embedding, nearby metadata,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.950
and index records deserve careful treatment as part of the data architecture.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.775
Vector stores may hold embeddings, chunk text, document IDs, tenant labels,

00:00:20.775 --> 00:00:23.910
permission tags, source references, and lifecycle metadata.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.720
They are not just performance caches.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.535
They are systems that influence which evidence reaches the model and which users can

00:00:29.535 --> 00:00:31.030
discover source-derived material.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.550
Collection and namespace design should follow data sensitivity, ownership,

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.360
tenant boundaries, and query patterns.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.985
Mixing unrelated tenants or sensitivity levels into one broad index can make permission

00:00:40.985 --> 00:00:43.000
enforcement and lifecycle management harder.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.955
Separate collections are not always required,

00:00:44.955 --> 00:00:47.915
but the design should make access rules clear and testable.

00:00:47.915 --> 00:00:49.600
Metadata filters should be deliberate.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.365
Tenant identifiers, classification labels, lifecycle status, source type, effective dates,

00:00:54.365 --> 00:00:57.070
and permission fields often become retrieval controls.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.360
If those fields are incomplete or inconsistent,

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.480
the retrieval layer may select content that is stale, irrelevant,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.080
or inappropriate for the workflow.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.125
Index refresh and deletion behavior matter.

00:01:06.125 --> 00:01:09.920
Documents change, permissions move, projects close, and old guidance expires.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.660
The architecture should define how indexes are rebuilt, updated, backed up, restored,

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:15.420
and purged.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.845
Administrative access to indexes should be protected because index operators may influence

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:22.725
retrieval behavior and source-derived data availability.

00:01:22.725 --> 00:01:26.930
Teams should document what each index contains, who owns it, how it is refreshed,

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.520
and what operational signals show that it is healthy.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.540
That record helps reviewers treat the vector store as a managed data system rather than a

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.120
hidden implementation detail.
